  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1120 PM CDT Sun May 10 2026\n\nA generally benign weather pattern is setting up for the upcoming\nweek as an upper-level ridge, currently anchored over the Four \nCorners, inches slowly eastward. A broad trough over the eastern \nCONUS this evening will be reinforced by a northern stream \nshortwave trough midweek, maintaining dry, northwest flow aloft \nacross the Mid-South. A shortwave ridge axis will be situated\nalong the MS River Valley by the end of the work week, but the \nprogression of this ridge signifies a potential pattern change \nheading into early next week.\n\nBacking up and looking at the finer details, a shortwave trough\nrounding the base of the longwave trough has promoted large-scale\nforcing for ascent over the Lower MS Valley this afternoon and\nevening. This has been responsible for the latest crop of showers\nand thunderstorms over the Mid-South. This feature will translate\neast overnight, but we will see occasional stratiform rain with \nembedded convection through sunrise Monday morning.\n\nGenerally rain-free conditions are anticipated Monday afternoon\nthrough Friday within this northwest flow aloft regime. A midweek\nsystem may bring some rain to the OH Valley on Tuesday, but this\nis likely to remain well to our north. The air mass will be\nunseasonably dry, with precipitable water flirting with the 10th\npercentile of climatology (near or even below 0.50\"). This will\nallow larger-than-normal diurnal temperature ranges through the\nwork week, with near to slightly above normal highs in the upper \n70s to mid 80s. Overnight lows will be below normal, dipping into \nthe 50s each morning, with some areas (especially those toward the\nTN River Valley) cooling into the upper 40s.\n\nThe dry air will promote low relative humidity values, but wind\nspeeds will generally stay in check. Current 10-hr fuel moisture\ncontent is 9-11% across the CWA, so we'll be watching for some\nmarginally elevated fire weather concerns. Temperatures will peak\nthis weekend as the upper-level ridge axis passes and southwest \nflow aloft promotes increasing southerly flow and warm advection. \nTemperatures at 850 mb are progged to warm as we move into the \nweekend, supporting surface temperatures at or above 90 degrees. \nJonesboro is the only official climate station for WFO Memphis \nthat has recorded a 90-degree temperature this year, but that may \nchange this weekend. The latest NBM probabilities for 90+ highs \nexceed 50% across most of the area, with some locations in eastern\nAR or West TN topping out at a 75% (or greater) chance.\n\nThe aforementioned pattern shift will bring the next chance for\nshowers and thunderstorms to the Mid-South early next week.",
